--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
dropped the body
--
N5008 [temp.inst]/10-11: overload resolution instantiates only the
DECLARATION of a function template specialization; its definition is
instantiated only when the specialization is selected and odr-used.
Resolving `p == q` considers `count`'s friend `operator==(const count &,
const count &)`, which probes a user-defined conversion of `point` to
`count` through the constructor template `explicit count(P)`.  That
candidate is never viable ([over.match.copy]/1: only converting, i.e.
non-explicit, constructors), yet the end-of-translation-unit sweeps
converted the speculative instance `count<point>`'s body, which is
ill-formed for a non-pointer P, and reported it as a dropped system-header
function.  libstdc++'s `__shared_count(_Ptr)` produced this for every
`operator==` operand type in a translation unit including <memory>
(`delete __p` on a non-pointer).
